So you can actually
scale numbers

00:11:36.265 --> 00:11:41.140
with the formatters in
gt, which is kind of cool.

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:42.473
Now we have this.

00:11:42.473 --> 00:11:44.890
We may notice there's a few
things that are kind of, like,

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:46.840
not great.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:47.740
Like, for one thing--

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.240
this probably just
bothers me-- but,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:53.770
like, the width of these
columns is not the same across.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:57.213
It's basically governed by the
width of the largest label.

00:11:57.213 --> 00:11:58.630
In this case,
flipper length makes

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:00.190
the column much more wider.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:01.510
But we can change that.

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:02.560
We can change anything.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:08.110
We can go to use this function
called cols_width right there.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.650
And what we can do is we
can select the columns.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:11.850
I believe it's going to be--

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.360
OK.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:19.130
You can use tySelect
on the left side.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:20.330
So we can do things like--

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.180
OK.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.050
So we can use this right here.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:26.890
We can use just this
bars list, I think.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:33.150
OK.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.960
Like so.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:40.120
We're going to bring that down
like this, because we just

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.930
need the names
itself, not in quotes.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.430
Oh, I apologize.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.740
We actually-- no, that's right.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:49.450
Those are the original names.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:50.305
So we get like this.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:55.630
And then on the
right side, we're

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:58.400
going to put in width value.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.925
So we can use this px
helper function if you want.

00:13:00.925 --> 00:13:02.800
And it just means that
any value put in there

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:03.970
is going to a mixed list.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.512
It's the same thing
as writing, like,

00:13:05.512 --> 00:13:08.690
in quotes some number with
px to the right of it.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:12.130
So let's put in, like, 50 to see
where it starts, and we'll just

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:16.420
copy that down to
each of these like so

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:19.640
and see what it gets us.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:20.300
No.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.498
OK, so I got that wrong.

00:13:21.498 --> 00:13:23.540
Instead of equals signs,
we should be using this.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:26.480
Great.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.070
OK, let's try this out.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.060
OK, not bad.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.910
I mean, a little bit
small, but the cool thing

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:38.110
is we can stretch this out.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:40.190
So you can make this
80, for instance.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:43.920
And then we get it, like, a
width that's kind of nice.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.310
Oh, not quite right.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:52.320
Let's make this even
bigger, 120, I think.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.070
There we go, and we may
even bring it down a bit.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:56.550
It's all, like, iterative.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.890
You're going to adjust
things as you go.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.810
Luckily, it's pretty fast.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.657
There's almost no time cost.

00:14:04.657 --> 00:14:06.240
I think we missed
the sweet spot here.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:08.948
I think it's going to be
like 110 or thereabouts

00:14:08.948 --> 00:14:10.365
where, like, things
stop wrapping.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.990
Maybe 120 was good.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:15.490
OK.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.573
I'm going to go with this.

00:14:16.573 --> 00:14:17.260
This is not bad.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.740
So now we have all the columns,
like, at least in the data part

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:24.520
being of the same width,
which is kind of nice.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.430
It kind of leaves your
eye in a nice way.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.260
Like, each of these
numbers are spaced out.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.470
They have similar speeds between
them, which is kind of cool.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:34.900
And you can also do the same
thing for the stub, as well.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.333
You just use it to
orient everything.

00:14:37.333 --> 00:14:39.250
And that means, in this
case, everything else.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:40.840
And we can make
that a bit bigger.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:45.015
I'm not sure what it's starting
at, but if you just do 60,

00:14:45.015 --> 00:14:46.640
it's definitely going
to change things.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.660
And I'm going the
wrong direction.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.930
Maybe you want that to be 100.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:52.340
And there we are.

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:56.660
Now we have an idea as our table
with the width that we want it.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.780
So we're basically focusing
on presentation here,

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.130
and we're trying to make
it look good, I guess.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.570
But these are tricks you
can do pretty simply.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.153
You just have to--
and, of course, you

00:15:06.153 --> 00:15:07.270
need to use tySelect here.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.260
You have a statement
that works nicely

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.820
for all these four columns.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.597
That works.

00:15:12.597 --> 00:15:14.180
You have to use on
the left side there

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:18.190
and just use px equals
120 for all of them.

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:19.660
So that's that.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:23.290
So say you want
to add some notes.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:27.068
And we add a pair section, but
what about a footnote section?

00:15:27.068 --> 00:15:28.360
So we can definitely have that.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.180
And there's two ways to
put stuff in the footer.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.860
We can use tab_source_note.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:34.360
Great.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:38.200
These are just general notes
that you pop into the bottom.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:42.280
And a good thing we can
say here, or put in,

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:44.323
is where the dataset comes from.

00:15:44.323 --> 00:15:45.050
OK.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:56.640
So dataset is from the
palmerpenguins package.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.868
And we can just say our package,
as well, and put a period.

00:16:00.868 --> 00:16:02.160
We're in a little bit of space.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:05.050
I'm going to just
line break here.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:08.235
And we can try that
out, but I know

00:16:08.235 --> 00:16:09.360
there's going be a mistake.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:13.640
And the mistake is that we're
using Markdown when, really, we

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:14.520
didn't declare it.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:18.020
So in all these parts, all these
opportunities to put in text,

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.690
we can definitely use
md helper function.

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:23.210
And it just means
interpret it as Markdown.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:27.620
And any output
format you use will

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:31.160
render that to be
looking nice like the way

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:32.870
you want it to look.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:37.724
And we may even do a thing like
accentuate r, just like this.

00:16:37.724 --> 00:16:39.810
Sort of tense up a bit.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:40.663
There we go.

00:16:40.663 --> 00:16:42.080
So that's just
like a source note.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:44.060
Basically, it's
a note that's not

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:47.198
tied into any particular
place in the table,

00:16:47.198 --> 00:16:47.990
but we can do that.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.017
We can use tab_footnote.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.390
And this takes in,
like, two arguments.

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.120
So in the previous one, you
just had to put in a note.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:04.319
In this case, you have to put it
in the note, plus what location

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:06.040
the note was referring to.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:08.280
So let's add the footnote first.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:11.310
And I guess now's a good time to
talk about what we can actually

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:12.349
point out.

00:17:12.349 --> 00:17:15.750
We can just say, like,
Gentoo looks to be,

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:19.410
like, the biggest
penguin by most accounts.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:23.040
I mean, not so much in
bill length and bill

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:26.410
depth, but certainly,
flipper length and body mass.

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:28.069
The Gentoo is,
like, reign supreme,

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:29.360
I guess, amongst these three.

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:30.560
So let's just do that.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:33.490
Let's just say, like,
Gentoo is largest.

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:42.780
I'll just say the largest of
the three penguins studied.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:43.280
Great.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.870
So that's great.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.890
That's the note that's
going to appear tomorrow.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:48.557
So where are we going
to attach this to?

00:17:48.557 --> 00:17:50.840
I mean, the most
logical place in my mind

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:53.340
would be, like, the label
itself or Gentoo right here.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:54.570
So in this step.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:18:01.070
So in locations, we can use this
huge amount of location helper

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:05.100
functions, and they
all begin with cells.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:07.470
And you see the auto pop,
this or the auto help,

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:11.260
shows us, gives us a guide as
to which ones are available.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.088
In this case, you
want cell_stub.

00:18:13.088 --> 00:18:14.880
So it's going to appear
a little bit lower.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:15.600
There we are.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:18.690
And if you just take a
look on the right side,

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:21.190
we got rows is
the only argument.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:25.430
So in this case, we want
to say rows equals Gentoo.

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:29.060
We just want to repeat the
label that's in the stub.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:29.900
OK.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:32.640
Great, so let's run this.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:33.335
Great.

00:18:33.335 --> 00:18:34.470
There's our first footnote.

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:35.230
It's really great.

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:36.990
So we have, as
footnotes often do,

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:42.070
they have some sort of
glyph, like for where

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.478
the note is referring to.

00:18:43.478 --> 00:18:44.770
And there's a note right there.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:47.170
So the footnotes, the
numbered footnotes, they

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:50.590
appear above the source
notes, and it's a cool thing.

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:52.990
We can also change the way the--

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:54.250
the glyphs themselves.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:58.090
We can use opt for note marks.

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.200
And we can say marks could be
something other than numbers.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.920
It could be letters.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.375
So if we do that,
we begin with A.

00:19:07.375 --> 00:19:12.130
And we can do capital
letters like so.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:16.960
And going further, we could
have a custom set of marks.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:22.360
So you might do
star, double star,

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:23.562
and who knows what you have?

00:19:23.562 --> 00:19:26.020
If you have any source set,
it'll just run through the set.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.700
So we can do that, as well.

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:28.570
There we go.

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:29.987
So you can really
change the marks

00:19:29.987 --> 00:19:33.550
to be whatever you want to
be, which is super nice.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:35.228
So that is the first footnote.

00:19:35.228 --> 00:19:36.770
I'm going to take
this part out, just

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:38.790
to demonstrate the
order of footnotes.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.850
So the cool thing
about gt is that you

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.740
don't have to manually
manage your footnotes.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:48.840
You can just go nuts with adding
tab_footnote, tab_footnote,

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.780
and gt will handle
the ordering and all

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.205
the other, sort
of, small details

00:19:53.205 --> 00:19:54.330
when it comes to footnotes.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.210
So we can say things like--

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:00.750
we can elaborate on what,
say, flipper length is.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:11.680
I mean, we can say in a
footnote, flipper length is--

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.430
I don't know the details
about the dataset

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:14.995
or how the data was acquired.

00:20:14.995 --> 00:20:16.370
But we can say
flipper length was

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.780
measured with a tape measure.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:20.150
Let's be a bit silly here.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:20.780
Maybe it was.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:21.500
Who knows?

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:23.400
This is maybe a
little bit of fiction.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:23.900
OK.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.180
So let's say that's true.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.180
Flipper length was measured
with a tape measure.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.380
So the note would appear
right here on the column label

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:32.330
itself, flipper length.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:33.355
So cool.

00:20:33.355 --> 00:20:33.980
We can do that.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.670
We could say locations
equals cells,

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:39.380
and autocomplete is
your friend here.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:41.390
It'll be cells_column_labels.

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:43.620
It just takes the
columns argument.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:45.590
And again, we just put
in the column name.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:47.007
So I'm going to
type that all out.

00:20:47.007 --> 00:20:51.830
Columns equals flipper.

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:56.558
Hit autocomplete again, and
we're going to run this.

00:20:56.558 --> 00:20:57.100
There we are.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.590
So before we had one
where Gentoo was,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:01.603
now it's number two,
because that appears later,

00:21:01.603 --> 00:21:02.520
which is kind of cool.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.145
We didn't have to,
sort of, match that.

00:21:04.145 --> 00:21:07.090
We didn't have to say
what the glyph was.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:10.900
Gt does a sensible thing
of, like, indexing footnotes

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:12.618
from top to bottom,
left to right,

00:21:12.618 --> 00:21:14.660
so we don't have to worry
about things like that.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:17.110
And here's another
really cool thing.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:21.170
Say, for instance, you wanted
two footnotes on the same spot.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:21.670
OK.

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:24.070
So tab_footnotes.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.410
So we'll add
additional note about--

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:27.310
this is very silly.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:29.560
I'm going to copy this whole
thing, because it's going

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:32.000
to be on the same location.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:43.690
We'll say something like, the
tape measure, unfortunately,

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:49.690
suffered some frost
damage, because they

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:52.780
were in the Antarctic,
so things happen.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:53.620
So yeah.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:57.160
Maybe they had to be,
like, disclose everything.

00:21:57.160 --> 00:21:57.660
OK.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:01.140
So now we have two notes
on the same column label.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:02.370
So what's going to happen?

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:03.870
It seems like some
sort of conflict,

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:05.100
but no, gt handle that.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.490
You can just run this.

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:07.650
We get one and two.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:11.365
So there's, basically, two
notes, two different notes.

00:22:11.365 --> 00:22:14.860
They alight to this same cell.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.670
And then Gentoo is number
three, which is great.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:19.470
But it'll handle multiple notes.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.570
And the cool thing
is you can even

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:25.590
have a list of
cell column labels,

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:28.510
or-- sorry-- of locations.

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:32.350
So we can have this note
being applied to more stuff.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:36.020
So you can say, cells_data.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:38.220
Or I think it's cells_body.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.970
And we want columns to be the
same column, flipper length.

00:22:44.483 --> 00:22:46.150
And basically, what
it's going to do now

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:49.690
is it's going to put the note,
the glyph, on the column label

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:53.540
and all of the values
in that column.

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:57.250
So if we run this,
you see that, too,

00:22:57.250 --> 00:23:03.040
is repeated down, because the
tape measure is compromised.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:06.960
So basically, we're just
putting the notes, the glyphs,

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:08.915
on the actual values
themselves, too.

00:23:08.915 --> 00:23:10.290
It's a bit silly,
but I just want

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:13.690
to show you that footnotes,
it's very flexible.

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:15.480
It does the right
thing most of the time.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:19.020
Nobody's ever complained
about it, which is good news.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:20.940
And I think people
actually like it,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:22.830
because they didn't
complain about it.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:24.210
So that is good.

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:26.310
So footnotes work,
is what I'm saying.

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:31.170
So what if you wanted to
highlight a certain bit of data

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:32.590
in this table?

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:33.090
OK.

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:34.620
We can totally do that, too.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.420
We can use tab_style.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:38.272
OK.

00:23:38.272 --> 00:23:39.730
And it operates
under the same sort

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:43.860
of principles as tab_footnote.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:47.110
I mean, it has locations, and
we use the same location number

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:48.290
functions.

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:50.950
So let's say, for instance,
for whatever reason,

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:56.140
we wanted highlight the entire
row of the Gentoo penguin, just

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.112
the data.

00:23:57.112 --> 00:23:57.820
I don't know why.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:23:59.070
But say you wanted to do that.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:00.400
OK, so we can do that.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.923
So we do cells_body.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:05.940
Great.

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.975
We use columns
equals everything.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:12.230
Great.

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:17.140
But in this case, you want rows
to be limited to just Gentoo.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:17.692
OK.

00:24:17.692 --> 00:24:19.150
This looks solid,
but it just means

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:22.450
that we're going to target
all the columns, but just

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:25.270
the rows and all those columns
that correspond to Gentoo.

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:26.770
And that, of course,
is in the stub.

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:28.520
When you have something
in the stub that's

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:31.900
an additional advantage, you
can use the rows argument more

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:32.620
precisely.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:34.900
You can actually
just use the labels,

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.940
provided that they're unique.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.770
And most of the time,
they are, otherwise

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:40.940
it would be a little
bit less value.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:41.880
I'd say.

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:42.380
OK, great.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:43.090
So we have that.

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:46.030
Now we have the
cells_body statement.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:46.540
Great.

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:48.207
So now what are we
going to do about it?

00:24:48.207 --> 00:24:49.960
We're going to,
actually, add a style.

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.490
Great.

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.600
So we have a bunch of
style helper functions.

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:55.750
So basically, tab_style
uses two different types

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:59.000
of helper functions, one
in locations, one style.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.810
In this case, it's
singular cell.

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:04.480
And in this case, you
want to fill the cells

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:06.310
with some sort of color.

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:09.640
There's a default, but I'm
going to go with color,

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:13.810
and I'm going to go with like
steel blue, if I can spell it.

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:15.290
There we go.

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:17.420
And let's just see this work.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:20.530
So what I'm expecting to
see is the Gentoo row,

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:24.620
just the data cells will have
that color in the background.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:25.370
And it worked.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:26.450
That's great.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.180
So now we have that right there.

00:25:29.180 --> 00:25:30.560
We can change the color.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.833
It accepts all color
names, which is great.

00:25:32.833 --> 00:25:34.250
Light blue, I
believe, is a color.

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:36.860
That looks quite a bit nicer.

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:41.720
And we can also use text
colors, if you know them.

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:42.980
So you can do--

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:44.538
I don't know them.

00:25:44.538 --> 00:25:45.080
Let's try it.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:47.190
Let's try some random one here.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:50.900
Let's see if this
gives us anything nice.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:25:51.410
Not bad.

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:52.760
I just guessed at that.

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:54.138
OK, that worked out pretty well.

00:25:54.138 --> 00:25:55.930
But if you wanted to
say, for instance, you

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:57.560
had too dark of a
color, and you wanted

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:01.010
to have maximized contrast,
like, make this the text white,

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:02.810
for instance, you
can really do that.

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:07.520
You can have multiple styles
for the same certain location.

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:09.890
So we can close down the list.

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:12.130
Cell_fill is that.

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:18.350
And now we can use cell_text
and change the color of the text

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:21.250
to be white.

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:22.300
We'll have this.

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:25.760
I'll just tie this up a little
bit so it looks a bit better.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:26.260
OK.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:28.840
So in this case, I have
the cell_fill color,

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:33.850
and then using a text color,
which is being changed.

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:35.110
Great.

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:35.740
Not too bad.

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:38.500
So now we have better contrast,
because we chose a darker

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:40.370
background color.

00:26:40.370 --> 00:26:41.740
So that is that.

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:45.295
So you can definitely do things
like style certain cells.

00:26:45.295 --> 00:26:46.420
This applies to everything.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.220
You can style the header.

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:49.390
You can style the footer.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:50.590
You can style to set stub.

00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:53.650
Every single location
here is stylable.

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:56.020
One more thing, just
to complete this table,

00:26:56.020 --> 00:26:59.680
we have this top left corner
here that's customarily empty.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:02.142
I mean, the default
is that it's empty.

00:27:02.142 --> 00:27:03.100
But we can change that.

00:27:03.100 --> 00:27:06.510
There's a special function
just for adding content

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:07.810
to that top left corner.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:09.730
It's called tab_stubhead.

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:11.980
And it just accepts the label.

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:17.780
And as we often do, we can use
Markdown to make that nice.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.090
And in this case, it's
kind of a wild card.

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:21.970
You can describe
either the columns.

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:23.140
You can describe the rows.

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:24.865
You can describe both.

00:27:24.865 --> 00:27:25.490
It's up to you.

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:26.948
I'm going to put
in penguin, so I'm

00:27:26.948 --> 00:27:28.670
going to treat it
more like a column.

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:30.400
So I'm just going to
say penguin species.

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:34.580
Great.

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:38.850
So I'm going to add that in,
and there it is in the top left.

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:42.588
So you can definitely use
that once empty corner.

00:27:42.588 --> 00:27:44.880
You just have to remember
the functioning tab_stubhead.

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:48.500
It's low on argument, so it's
not too difficult to use.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:49.748
You'll get it.

00:27:49.748 --> 00:27:51.290
And that's my nice
table, and there's

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:54.008
tons and tons of options.

00:27:54.008 --> 00:27:55.800
So say you wanted to
change the table font.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:59.307
You can do that with this opt
function called opt_table_font.

00:27:59.307 --> 00:28:01.890
This is a pretty popular option
because, sometimes the default

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:03.345
font is not what you want.

00:28:03.345 --> 00:28:05.220
And the great thing
about this opt_table_font

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:07.620
is you could use local fonts.

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:10.510
Say you have Helvetica
on your machine.

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.620
You can totally use that,
and it changes the look.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.780
Let's say you want something
that is not on your machine,

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:18.090
maybe part of Google Fonts.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:23.050
You could use google_font,
then choose a font

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.220
from their service, as
long as you know the name.

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:27.250
It's not too hard to do.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:29.420
So Montserrat is one of them.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:32.170
Now let's use that.

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:32.670
Great.

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:33.628
And it totally changed.

00:28:33.628 --> 00:28:35.230
And we have all sorts
of options here.

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:37.320
We have things like the
weight can be changed.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:38.820
Usually, these are
numbered weights,

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:44.770
so we can change it to be
heavier, like, say, 600.

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:45.460
There we go.

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:48.500
So we definitely see that now.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:50.770
And you'd have to go to
the Google Fonts page

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:54.610
to see what weight
ranges are available.

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.070
But that can be totally used.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:28:58.510
There's options,
too, like styles.

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:01.960
You can choose, like, italic,
oblique, things like that.

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:04.800
Let's actually do that,
see if that works.

00:29:07.105 --> 00:29:07.605
There we go.

00:29:07.605 --> 00:29:09.855
So everything was changed
to, like, the italic version

00:29:09.855 --> 00:29:11.210
of the font.

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:13.210
So what I'm going to do
is I'm going to take out

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:15.160
this tab_style thing for now.

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:17.050
I'm going to comment
it out, because I'm

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:21.020
going to apply our own
style based on data color.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:21.520
OK.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:25.210
So data color-- this is what I
do every time I use data color.

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:28.630
It's really hard to
use, because it requires

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:31.390
that you use functions
within the function,

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.390
like function calls, and
they're not even gt functions.

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:36.530
They're functions from
the scales package.

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:39.730
So I would always use the help.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.730
I'll go to data color, like so.

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:46.840
And then, of course, it
tells you what it does.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:49.450
And like all the help
files, it gives you

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:52.090
the visuals, showing
what it does,

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:53.740
which are paired
to the examples.

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:54.267
But yeah.

00:29:54.267 --> 00:29:56.350
My point is, I would go
to the example that works,

00:29:56.350 --> 00:29:59.600
and then just take
it and go from there.

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:01.435
So in this case,
what we have here is,

00:30:01.435 --> 00:30:04.780
it's working on a single column.

00:30:04.780 --> 00:30:06.760
And we have the colors argument.

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:10.263
It requires a function col.
Typically, it's from scales,

00:30:10.263 --> 00:30:11.680
and the help tells
you about this.

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:13.540
But I'm just going
to copy this in,

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.280
because it's much
easier to show than it

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:17.865
is to explain it beforehand.

00:30:17.865 --> 00:30:19.240
And what it's
going to do-- we're

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.520
going to adapt this to
particular example here.

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:23.020
So if you look at
your viewer again,

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:24.270
we have these ones right here.

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:28.980
We have columns right there.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:34.610
So I'm going to say columns are
going to be the same as before.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:35.610
So I'm going to choose--

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.180
maybe for the sake
of brevity, I'm

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:44.000
going to choose just two
columns, bill length and bill

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.480
depth.

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:47.320
Great.

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.798
So now, the colors will be--

00:30:49.798 --> 00:30:51.340
will basically be
colorized according

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:52.960
to the values in the columns.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:53.770
That's great.

00:30:53.770 --> 00:30:56.860
So we're going to use
col_numeric to create our,

00:30:56.860 --> 00:31:00.100
sort of, like, set
of colors that we're

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:01.900
going to interpolate through.

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:04.660
So the range is going
to be the domain.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:07.360
And we can have this being null,
and that just sets the domain

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:09.092
to the range of the values.

00:31:09.092 --> 00:31:11.050
But I kind of like actually
signing the domain.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:15.980
So I'm going to use
c equals 30, and then

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:17.893
50 for the upper
part of the range.

00:31:17.893 --> 00:31:20.060
And then I'm going to set,
like, maybe three colors,

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:22.640
because it's going to
be like 30, 40, and 50.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:26.570
So I'm just going to choose
red, orange, green and omit

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:29.430
the blue.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.030
Great, great.

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:33.190
So let's see if
this actually works.

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:35.740
The key thing is will
this actually work.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:36.510
OK.

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:38.692
And it does sort of work.

00:31:38.692 --> 00:31:40.150
The reason why it
doesn't work here

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:42.340
is because this is
actually out of range.

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:44.772
So if you want to have
different domains,

00:31:44.772 --> 00:31:47.230
we can definitely do that with
multiple cols of data color,

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:52.450
or we can set this
to null, like that.

00:31:52.450 --> 00:31:53.950
And again, this
domain is, actually,

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:58.010
inside the scale's
col_numeric function call.

00:31:58.010 --> 00:32:01.055
So a little tricky, but when
it works, it works awesomely.

00:32:01.055 --> 00:32:02.680
So this will be nice,
because this will

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:06.608
treat each column separately.

00:32:06.608 --> 00:32:07.150
Actually, no.

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:09.580
It treats it holistically.

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:13.570
So but the point being, if
you just had, say, like,

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:17.290
bill length here, this
would be much more nicer.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.200
Then we can actually
choose the domains

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:22.750
that make more sense for
the particular type of data

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:23.830
we have in there.

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:27.080
Let's do 30, 50.

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:27.840
There we go.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.410
So we can see here
that red is not really

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:32.870
going to be in the picture,
because we're pretty far

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:33.920
away from 30.

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:36.800
We're getting more close to
orange, which is sitting at 40,

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:40.190
and then green sits at 50,
which we do see quite a bit

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:42.260
of in Chinstrap and Gentoo.

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:44.150
But now that we
have a template, we

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:46.430
can actually do this for
each and every column,

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:49.020
just tweaking the
values of domain.

00:32:49.020 --> 00:32:51.590
So for instance, we want
to do this for bill depth.

00:32:54.760 --> 00:33:02.070
So we could change this to
be something like 10 to 20.

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:06.600
And we're going to see
that, as well, basically.

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:07.740
There we are.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:09.130
So, so on and so forth.

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:10.560
It does that.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:12.690
You can choose even
super dark colors, like,

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:15.390
if you wanted to have
brown, for instance.

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:16.200
What will it do?

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:20.308
I think it will actually
reverse the text if the color is

00:33:20.308 --> 00:33:21.600
like-- it will do that for you.

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:26.010
It will, essentially, like, look
at contrast and say, like, oh.

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:27.820
This background color
is a bit too dark.

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:30.060
It's going to flip
the text color around.

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:32.550
And you can also disable that,
but it's just sort of there.

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:35.133
You can override that, as well,
with your own sort of styling,

00:33:35.133 --> 00:33:37.905
but it's just a thing that
data color does for you.

00:33:37.905 --> 00:33:39.780
And so that's a way you
can, sort of, make a,

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:42.607
sort of, like, heat map
for your table values.

00:33:42.607 --> 00:33:44.190
And you can keep
going on, and there's

00:33:44.190 --> 00:33:45.525
more functions in col_numeric.

00:33:45.525 --> 00:33:47.040
There's col_factor.

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:48.360
It's all in help here.

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:54.350
Quantiles numeric, you know,
anything you want, you got it.

00:33:54.350 --> 00:33:56.620
It's pretty nice.

